  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "or9E5sYAKih/u1x8q87Pkv6KnhA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:13.991Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The demo starts from an event Magenta's own product already emits. When an organisational change happens in OS2mo, that change is dispatched as a typed operation, so what gets stored is an attributed, replayable record of the change rather than the new state of a row. No new trigger has to be invented for the seam to exist.\n\nThe distinctive part of this design is not the technical seam but the route to adoption. Proposing the integration through the OS2 cooperative means it is evaluated as shared municipal infrastructure, not as one client's customisation. The artefact and the channel are chosen together: the same adapter, offered privately, would be a single project.\n\nCommercially that is the whole point. A per-client integration has to be re-sold at every engagement, while something the cooperative adopts is specified once and inherited by its members. The research selects the cooperative route explicitly for that reason, which makes this demo as much a distribution decision as an engineering one.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Magenta ApS (DK Copenhagen + GL) \u2014 Morten Kj\u00e6rsgaard, CEO \u00b7 info@magenta.dk \u00b7 https://magenta.dk\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> OS2mo organisational-change events dispatched as typed operations, proposed through the OS2 cooperative so it lands as shared municipal infrastructure rather than one client project.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:24:13.991244Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:13.991249Z"
    }
  },
